Dr. Richard S. Wallace of the A.L.I.C.E. AI Foundation of San Francisco
will be speaking at the Linux Users' Group of Davis in Davis, California.

Dr. Wallace is the inventor of the A.L.I.C.E. chat robot
(Artificial Linguistic Internet Computer Entity), the author of
AIML (Artificial Intelligence Markup Languge) and Chairman of the Board of
the ALICE A.I. Foundation, a non-profit research organization.

More then 500 developers from around the world have contributed to the
open source A.L.I.C.E. project. The program won the Loebner Prize, an
annual Turing Test, in 2000 and 2001. Numerous startup and established
companies have embraced the AIML standard for chat robot development,
and are creating new AIML tools, entertainment bots, customer service bots,
and avatars using our technology.

The talk will include a technical overview of AIML, along with examples
showing the use of each tag.

Dr. Wallace will describe the history the research that led to A.L.I.C.E.,
beginning with Turing's 1950 paper. The design of AIML owes much to the
original ELIZA psychiatrist program, but ELIZA had only 200 question and
answer pairs, whereas A.L.I.C.E. has over 40,000.

Artificial intelligence has been dogged by outrageous hoaxes and
deceptions. A.L.I.C.E. and AIML is probably the first technology to
embrace this tradition openly. The art of writing AIML involves creating
responses that fool the client into thinking that the robot really
understands.

 The talk concludes with some philosophical questions about consciousness.
Is consciousness a mere anthropocentric illusion, like Ptolemy's solar
system? Are we anything more than sophisticated robots like A.L.I.C.E.?
Is there a difference between the illusion of consciousness, and the
"real thing"?


For more information, visit:

  http://www.lugod.org/meeting/upcoming/#2002.03.18


-bill!
pr@lugod.org
http://www.lugod.org/

